<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Improved probabilistic regression using
<br class="ltx_break">diffusion models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Carlo Kneissl<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christopher Bülte<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Philipp Scholl
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal contribution.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Munich Center for Machine Learning (MCML)
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Munich, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{kneissl, buelte, scholl}@math.lmu.de</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gitta Kutyniok
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Tromsø
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>DLR-German Aerospace Center
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Munich Center for Machine Learning (MCML)
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Munich, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kutyniok@math.lmu.de</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Probabilistic regression models the entire predictive distribution of a response variable, offering richer insights than classical point estimates and directly allowing for uncertainty quantification. While diffusion-based generative models have shown remarkable success in generating complex, high-dimensional data, their usage in general regression tasks often lacks uncertainty-related evaluation and remains limited to domain-specific applications. We propose a novel diffusion-based framework for probabilistic regression that learns predictive distributions in a nonparametric way. More specifically, we propose to model the full distribution of the diffusion noise, enabling adaptation to diverse tasks and enhanced uncertainty quantification. We investigate different noise parameterizations, analyze their trade-offs, and evaluate our framework across a broad range of regression tasks, covering low- and high-dimensional settings. For several experiments, our approach shows superior performance against existing baselines, while delivering calibrated uncertainty estimates, demonstrating its versatility as a tool for probabilistic prediction.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id6" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Supervised regression concerns predicting a response variable <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> from covariates <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{c}\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{c}\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Classical approaches estimate the conditional mean <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\bm{y}\mid\bm{c}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\bm{y}\mid\bm{c}]</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas probabilistic regression models the full predictive distribution <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bishop, 1994</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and Meinshausen, 2024</a>)</cite>. The latter provides calibrated uncertainty estimates and captures complex data-generating processes, including multimodal outcomes and nonlinear noise. We adopt a broad notion of regression, encompassing heterogeneous data types such as scalars, images, and trajectories. A key modeling choice is the specification of <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math>: while Gaussian assumptions remain widespread <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lakshminarayanan et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nix and Weigend, 1994</a>)</cite>, recent work has emphasized more flexible non-parametric and distribution-free alternatives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kelen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and Meinshausen, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion-based generative models have emerged as state-of-the-art approaches for high-dimensional data generation, achieving remarkable results in tasks such as photorealistic image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and video synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These models are typically formulated as diffusion probabilistic models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, where data are gradually perturbed by Gaussian noise (the forward process) and new samples are generated via the time-reversed dynamics. Prior work has advanced diffusion models through improved noise schedules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nichol and Dhariwal, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and sampling algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where the focus has largely been on accelerating the diffusion process while achieving a high generational quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nichol and Dhariwal, 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, diffusion models have been applied to various regression tasks such as depth estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, autoregressive flow prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohl et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Finzi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and weather forecasting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Price et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Couairon et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, often achieving state-of-the-art performance. Despite their inherently probabilistic nature, evaluations rarely emphasize uncertainty-related metrics or calibration. Recent efforts that aim to extract uncertainty estimates from diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shu and Farimani, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berry et al., 2024</a>)</cite> typically rely on training multiple networks, incurring substantial computational overhead. Furthermore, the intimate relation between uncertainty quantification and the noise modeling within the diffusion process remains underexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions: </span>In this work, we address these limitations by adapting the diffusion process to yield calibrated probabilistic predictions. Building on recent advances from generative modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we introduce a novel loss for diffusion-based regression models that enables learning a flexible noise distribution, moving beyond optimization of the conditional mean. We derive several trainable parameterizations that offer task-specific trade-offs between expressivity and computational efficiency while admitting closed-form sampling at the same time. These extensions provide principled epistemic uncertainty estimation within diffusion models, and we extensively validate our method across diverse regression tasks—including the UCI benchmark, flow prediction, weather forecasting, and depth estimation—showing consistent improvement in predictive performance and substantial gains in uncertainty quantification and calibration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Probabilistic regression</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}\in\mathcal{Y}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}\in\mathcal{Y}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the response variables of interest and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{c}\in\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{c}\in\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding conditioning variables. Classical regression focuses on estimating the conditional mean <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\bm{y}\mid\bm{c}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\bm{y}\mid\bm{c}]</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas probabilistic regression seeks to model the full conditional distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby explicitly capturing predictive uncertainty. Given training data <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}={(\bm{c}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}={(\bm{c}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal is to recover the predictive distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c},\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c},\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The setting is deliberately chosen to be general, including classical regression approaches, but also autoregressive prediction tasks, for example, when choosing <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{y}=\bm{y}_{t+1},\bm{c}=\bm{y}_{t},\ \bm{y}_{t}\in\mathcal{Y},\ \forall t=1,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}=\bm{y}_{t+1},\bm{c}=\bm{y}_{t},\ \bm{y}_{t}\in\mathcal{Y},\ \forall t=1,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>. A common strategy is to parameterize the conditional distribution via a generative mapping</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathcal{C}\times\mathcal{Z}\to\mathcal{Y},\quad\theta\in\Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{p}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathcal{C}\times\mathcal{Z}\to\mathcal{Y},\quad\theta\in\Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}\sim p_{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}\sim p_{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a tractable prior, typically Gaussian. The parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> are then optimized such that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}(\bm{c},\cdot)\approx p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\bm{c},\cdot)\approx p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we depart from classical parameterizations and instead employ diffusion-based generative models to represent <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c},\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\bm{y}\mid\bm{c},\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math>. For notational simplicity, we omit the explicit dependence on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> in the following.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Diffusion models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion probabilistic models (DPMs) aim to learn a target distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> from samples by estimating the reverse dynamics of a diffusion process. We follow the non-Markovian formulation of denoising diffusion implicit models (DDIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{1:T}\in[0,1]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1:T}\in[0,1]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a noise schedule. Define <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}\coloneqq 1-\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}\coloneqq 1-\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{t}\coloneqq\prod_{i=1}^{t}\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{t}\coloneqq\prod_{i=1}^{t}\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward process</em> is specified as
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{1:T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\coloneq p(\bm{x}_{T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\prod_{t=2}^{T}p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{1:T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\coloneq p(\bm{x}_{T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\prod_{t=2}^{T}p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math>
where, for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\underbrace{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\bm{x}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\cdot\frac{\bm{x}_{t}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{x}_{0}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}}_{\eqcolon\bm{\mu}(\bm{x}_{0},\bm{x}_{t})},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≕</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}(\underbrace{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\bm{x}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\cdot\frac{\bm{x}_{t}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{x}_{0}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}}_{\eqcolon\bm{\mu}(\bm{x}_{0},\bm{x}_{t})},\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\coloneq\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{T}}\bm{x}_{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{T})\mathbf{I}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\coloneq\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{T}}\bm{x}_{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{T})\mathbf{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and require <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to be sufficiently small such that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\approx\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{T}\mid\bm{x}_{0})\approx\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The variance schedule is parameterized as
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\coloneq\eta\sqrt{\tilde{\beta}}\coloneq\eta\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\beta_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msqrt></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\coloneq\eta\sqrt{\tilde{\beta}}\coloneq\eta\sqrt{\frac{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\beta_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> interpolating between the deterministic DDIM process (<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0</annotation></semantics></math>) and the stochastic DDPM process (<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An important feature of this construction is that for all choices of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and for all <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the property</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{x}_{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{t})\mathbf{I}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{0})=\mathcal{N}\left(\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{x}_{0},(1-\bar{\alpha}_{t})\mathbf{I}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which yields the identity</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}_{t}=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{X}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\epsilon_{t},\quad\epsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bf{I})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}_{t}=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{X}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\epsilon_{t},\quad\epsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bf{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> independent from <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by adding a specified amount of noise <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to the original input <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To generate samples, one must approximate the reverse process <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is intractable in general. DPMs approximate it with a latent-variable model</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{0:T})\coloneqq p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{T})\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{0:T})\coloneqq p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{T})\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which is a Markov chain that samples from <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is referred to as the <em id="S2.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generative process</em>. For sufficiently small <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the reverse transition <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is well approximated by a Gaussian, thus allowing to set <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{T})\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{T})\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and specifying the latent variable model as a neural network, <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}|\bm{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t-1};\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}|\bm{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t-1};\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Training proceeds by minimizing the variational lower bound (VLB), which reduces to a KL divergence between <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and the true posterior <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t},\bm{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. DDIM fixes the covariance to <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}=\sigma_{t}\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}=\sigma_{t}\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, while DDPM considers <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}\in\{\beta_{t}\mathbf{I},\tilde{\beta}_{t}\mathbf{I}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}\in\{\beta_{t}\mathbf{I},\tilde{\beta}_{t}\mathbf{I}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. With these simplifications, the VLB objective reduces (after reweighting) to a mean-squared error:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{simple}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,x_{0},\epsilon_{t}}\left[\lVert\epsilon_{t}-\epsilon_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\rVert_{2}^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>simple</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{simple}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,x_{0},\epsilon_{t}}\left[\lVert\epsilon_{t}-\epsilon_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\rVert_{2}^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To perform conditional modeling with covariates <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the target distribution is set as <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{0}=p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding an approximate predictive distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})\approx p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})\approx p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity, we omit explicit conditioning in the notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">While DDPM and DDIM have shown great success in conditional and unconditional generative modeling, note that due to the objective in Equation (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2 Diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the latent variable model only learns to approximate <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\approx\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\approx\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> and does not capture information about the full distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S2.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" style="width:1.0pt;height:0.21105912pt;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" version="1.1" width="1.33" height="0.28" overflow="visible"><g transform="translate(0,0.28) scale(1,-1)"><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,96.70) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="458.28" height="96.70" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04583v1/x1.svg" id="S2.F1.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="476" height="101" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.65,0.15)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="18.73" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="x_{0}" text="x _ 0" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m1">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m1.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m1.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">x</XMTok>
                          <XMTok fontsize="70%" meaning="0" role="NUMBER">0</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.05,0.15)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="21.12" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="x_{T}" text="x _ T" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m2">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m2.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m2.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">x</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">T</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.34,0.15)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="17.40" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="x_{t}" text="x _ t" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m3">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m3.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m3.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">x</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,96.70) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="458.28" height="96.70" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04583v1/x2.svg" id="S2.F1.pic1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="476" height="101" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.44,0.22)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="41.05" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\epsilon" text="square-root@(1 - (bar@(alpha)) _ t) * epsilon" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m4">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m4.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m4.1.1">
                          <XMTok meaning="times" role="MULOP">⁢</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m4.1.1.2">
                            <XMTok meaning="square-root"/>
                            <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m4.1.1.2.2">
                              <XMTok meaning="minus" role="ADDOP">-</XMTok>
                              <XMTok meaning="1" role="NUMBER">1</XMTok>
                              <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m4.1.1.2.2.3">
                                <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post2"/>
                                <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m4.1.1.2.2.3.2">
                                  <XMTok name="bar" role="OVERACCENT" stretchy="false">¯</XMTok>
                                  <XMTok font="italic" name="alpha" role="UNKNOWN">α</XMTok>
                                </XMApp>
                                <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                              </XMApp>
                            </XMApp>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok font="italic" name="epsilon" role="UNKNOWN">ϵ</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.1,0.26)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="43.30" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{T}}\epsilon" text="square-root@(1 - (bar@(alpha)) _ T) * epsilon" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m5">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m5.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m5.1.1">
                          <XMTok meaning="times" role="MULOP">⁢</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m5.1.1.2">
                            <XMTok meaning="square-root"/>
                            <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m5.1.1.2.2">
                              <XMTok meaning="minus" role="ADDOP">-</XMTok>
                              <XMTok meaning="1" role="NUMBER">1</XMTok>
                              <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m5.1.1.2.2.3">
                                <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post2"/>
                                <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m5.1.1.2.2.3.2">
                                  <XMTok name="bar" role="OVERACCENT" stretchy="false">¯</XMTok>
                                  <XMTok font="italic" name="alpha" role="UNKNOWN">α</XMTok>
                                </XMApp>
                                <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">T</XMTok>
                              </XMApp>
                            </XMApp>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok font="italic" name="epsilon" role="UNKNOWN">ϵ</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.36,0.01)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="53.40" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math class="ltx_math_unparsed" mode="inline" tex="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid x_{T})" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUPERSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1.1.1"/>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1.1.2">
                            <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1.1.2.1"/>
                            <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">p</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" name="theta" role="UNKNOWN">θ</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" name="epsilon" role="UNKNOWN">ϵ</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                        <XMWrap xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1.2">
                          <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                          <XMTok name="cdot" role="MULOP">⋅</XMTok>
                          <XMTok name="mid" role="VERTBAR">∣</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1.2.4">
                            <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m6.1.2.4.1"/>
                            <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">x</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">T</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                        </XMWrap>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.51,0.06)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="53.40" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math class="ltx_math_unparsed" mode="inline" tex="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid x_{t})" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUPERSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1.1.1"/>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1.1.2">
                            <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1.1.2.1"/>
                            <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">p</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" name="theta" role="UNKNOWN">θ</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" name="epsilon" role="UNKNOWN">ϵ</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                        <XMWrap xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1.2">
                          <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                          <XMTok name="cdot" role="MULOP">⋅</XMTok>
                          <XMTok name="mid" role="VERTBAR">∣</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1.2.4">
                            <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m7.1.2.4.1"/>
                            <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">x</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                        </XMWrap>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,96.70) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="458.28" height="96.70" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04583v1/x3.svg" id="S2.F1.pic1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="476" height="101" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(0.81,0.26)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="53.40" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math class="ltx_math_unparsed" mode="inline" tex="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid x_{t})" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUPERSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1.1.1"/>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1.1.2">
                            <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1.1.2.1"/>
                            <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">p</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" name="theta" role="UNKNOWN">θ</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" name="epsilon" role="UNKNOWN">ϵ</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                        <XMWrap xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1.2">
                          <XMTok role="OPEN" stretchy="false">(</XMTok>
                          <XMTok name="cdot" role="MULOP">⋅</XMTok>
                          <XMTok name="mid" role="VERTBAR">∣</XMTok>
                          <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1.2.4">
                            <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m8.1.2.4.1"/>
                            <XMTok font="italic" role="UNKNOWN">x</XMTok>
                            <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                          </XMApp>
                          <XMTok role="CLOSE" stretchy="false">)</XMTok>
                        </XMWrap>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(1.2,0.01)"><g transform="translate(0,15.94) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="18.86" height="11.16" overflow="visible"><Math mode="inline" tex="\epsilon_{t}" text="epsilon _ t" xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m9">
                      <XMath xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m9.1">
                        <XMApp xml:id="S2.F1.pic1.m9.1.1">
                          <XMTok role="SUBSCRIPTOP" scriptpos="post1"/>
                          <XMTok font="italic" name="epsilon" role="UNKNOWN">ϵ</XMTok>
                          <XMTok font="italic" fontsize="70%" role="UNKNOWN">t</XMTok>
                        </XMApp>
                      </XMath>
                    </Math></foreignObject></g></g><g transform="translate(1.22,0.22)"></g><g transform="translate(1.22,0.18)"></g><g transform="translate(1.22,0.13)"></g><g transform="translate(1.22,0.09)"></g><g transform="translate(0,0)"><g transform="translate(0,96.70) scale(1,-1)"><foreignObject width="458.28" height="96.70" overflow="visible"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04583v1/x4.svg" id="S2.F1.pic1.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" width="476" height="101" aria-label="Refer to caption"></object></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Method overview. At any given time <math id="S2.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> in the diffusion process, we need a prediction of the noise <math id="S2.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> given our current state <math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Traditionally, this is achieved by a network that approximates the conditional mean <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\epsilon_{t}\mid x_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\epsilon_{t}\mid x_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which, when viewed under a probabilistic viewpoint, treats the distribution <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as a Dirac distribution. We propose to model this distribution by a Gaussian Mixture family.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Our methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to improve uncertainty quantification in regression tasks with diffusion models by generalizing the diffusion loss, thereby enabling more accurate approximation of the conditional distribution <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Learning the full distribution in each denoising step</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most diffusion frameworks, following DDPM, fix the variance of the noise distribution in each denoising step. This design was originally motivated by two observations: (i) learning the variance often destabilized training, and (ii) variance modeling showed little benefit for image generation benchmarks, for example, with respect to the FID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heusel et al. (2018)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, subsequent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nichol and Dhariwal, 2021</a>)</cite> demonstrated that learning the variance improves likelihood estimates, indicating that recovering only the mean is insufficient for faithfully approximating the conditional distribution. Furthermore, the Gaussian approximation of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is only valid when the number of timesteps <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is large. Yet, large <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is computationally costly, and recent results suggest that using as few as 20–50 steps can yield superior performance in regression tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohl et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Price et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, particularly with improved noise schedulers and solvers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These observations motivate us to go beyond estimating the first two moments and instead learn the full distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, we reinterpret <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>: rather than treating it as a point estimate of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we view it as a random variable. This perspective naturally suggests replacing the mean-squared error loss <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{VLB}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>VLB</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{VLB}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a criterion that compares probability distributions rather than point predictions. To this end, we adopt the framework of strictly proper scoring rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dawid et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pacchiardi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex set of probability measures on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. A scoring rule <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathbb{P},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathbb{P},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> is a function that measures the discrepancy between a predictive distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and an observation <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gneiting and Raftery, 2007</a>)</cite>. The expected score is defined as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q})\coloneq\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\mathbb{Q}}[S(\mathbb{P},Y)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q})\coloneq\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\mathbb{Q}}[S(\mathbb{P},Y)]</annotation></semantics></math>. A scoring rule is called <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper</em> if <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathbb{Q},\mathbb{Q})\leq S(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathbb{Q},\mathbb{Q})\leq S(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P},\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <em id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strictly proper</em> if equality holds if and only if <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}=\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, strictly proper scoring rules ensure that the true distribution is the unique optimum and have been successfully applied in training neural networks for various tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pacchiardi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bülte et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Motivated by this, we propose the loss</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{SR}=\mathbb{E}_{t,x_{0},\epsilon_{t}}[S(p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid\bm{x}_{t}),\epsilon_{t})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{SR}=\mathbb{E}_{t,x_{0},\epsilon_{t}}[S(p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid\bm{x}_{t}),\epsilon_{t})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is a neural network–based model of the predictive distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a strictly proper scoring rule. This objective enables learning general noise distributions while ensuring that training accounts for the entire distribution rather than just its moments. 
<br class="ltx_break">Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Theoretical justification ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> connects this formulation to recent theoretical advances that justify our approach, and Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Parametrization of 𝑝_𝜃^ϵ(⋅∣𝒙_𝑡) ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> details design choices for both the noise distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and scoring rule <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Theoretical justification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrent work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al. (2025)</a></cite> reached a similar conclusion, proposing the use of proper scoring rules as a principled way to learn the full posterior distribution over noisy samples. Consider the reverse transition</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{0},\bm{x}_{t})\,p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})\,d\bm{x}_{0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}p(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{0},\bm{x}_{t})\,p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})\,d\bm{x}_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the intractable posterior <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> must be approximated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In standard DDPM/DDIM frameworks, this posterior is replaced by a point mass,
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})\approx\delta_{\hat{\bm{x}_{0}}(t,\bm{x}_{t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})\approx\delta_{\hat{\bm{x}_{0}}(t,\bm{x}_{t})}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}(t,\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}(t,\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the prediction of the denoising network. Trained with a regression loss, the denoiser recovers
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}(t,\bm{x}_{t})\approx\mathbb{E}[X_{0}\mid X_{t}=\bm{x}_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}(t,\bm{x}_{t})\approx\mathbb{E}[X_{0}\mid X_{t}=\bm{x}_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the DDIM formulation.
For <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, this procedure recovers the data distribution. However, when using only a small number of timesteps—a setting of practical interest—the approximation may no longer hold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al. (2025)</a></cite> proposed learning the full posterior distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> via proper scoring rules. They mainly focus on <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalized kernel scores</em>, which are of the form</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\lambda,\rho}(p,\bm{y})=\mathbb{E}_{p}[\rho(X,\bm{y})]-\frac{\lambda}{2}\mathbb{E}_{p\otimes p}[\rho(X,X^{\prime})]-\frac{1}{2}\rho(\bm{y},\bm{y})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\lambda,\rho}(p,\bm{y})=\mathbb{E}_{p}[\rho(X,\bm{y})]-\frac{\lambda}{2}\mathbb{E}_{p\otimes p}[\rho(X,X^{\prime})]-\frac{1}{2}\rho(\bm{y},\bm{y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">for kernel <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Special cases include the <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">energy score</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gneiting and Raftery, 2007</a>)</cite> for
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\bm{x}^{\prime},\bm{x})=\|\bm{x}^{\prime}-\bm{x}\|^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\bm{x}^{\prime},\bm{x})=\|\bm{x}^{\prime}-\bm{x}\|^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in(0,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in(0,2)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(Gaussian) kernel score</em> with
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho(\bm{x}^{\prime},\bm{x})=-\exp(-\|\bm{x}-\bm{x}^{\prime}\|^{2}/\gamma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\bm{x}^{\prime},\bm{x})=-\exp(-\|\bm{x}-\bm{x}^{\prime}\|^{2}/\gamma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Importantly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al. (2025)</a></cite> show that both recover the classical diffusion regression loss in the limit when combining (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.1 Learning the full distribution in each denoising step ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) with (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.2 Theoretical justification ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), a property termed <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">diffusion compatibility</em>. Furthermore, even for scoring rules lacking this property, the authors report substantial empirical gains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A second design choice concerns the parametrization of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and Meinshausen (2024)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose to concatenate Gaussian noise to the neural network input, thereby generating <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> samples of the target distribution. Training is then carried out using an unbiased estimator of Equation (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.2 Theoretical justification ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). This strategy provides a flexible, nonparametric estimate of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}_{0}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, but at significant computational cost: multiple samples <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> increase both the input dimensionality and runtime. Empirically, training is reported to be <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.3\times</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times</annotation></semantics></math> slower than standard diffusion models, even for moderate <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and may become prohibitive for large-scale datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Parametrization of <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As an alternative to nonparametric sampling-based approaches, we propose to model <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\cdot\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> directly through a parametrized distribution. This aligns naturally with scoring rule minimization, since closed-form expressions for the training objective are available. A schematic overview is given in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 Diffusion models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Depending on the choice of parametrization, one obtains a trade-off between computational efficiency and flexibility, which may vary across tasks: while simple Gaussian models may suffice in some applications, more complex structures are beneficial for multimodal or correlated noise patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we consider the general Gaussian mixture form</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}\mathcal{N}(\epsilon_{t};\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}\mathcal{N}(\epsilon_{t};\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with component means <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, positive-definite covariance matrices <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and mixture weights <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta,k}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta,k}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. From this general specification, we highlight three concrete parametrizations that offer a trade-off between the expressivity of the distribution and the simplicity of the model training:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Univariate Gaussian:</span> For <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t)),\ \sigma_{\theta}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t)),\ \sigma_{\theta}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a univariate Gaussian parametrization for each coordinate in the noise space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Univariate Gaussian mixture.</span>
Setting <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}(\bm{x}_{t},t)=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{\theta,k}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}(\bm{x}_{t},t)=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{\theta,k}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t))</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\theta,k}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\theta,k}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> enables multimodal modeling, as Gaussian mixtures can approximate arbitrary continuous densities to arbitrary precision under mild assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bishop, 1994</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Plataniotis and Hatzinakos, 2000</a>)</cite>, offering significantly greater expressivity at moderate cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multivariate Gaussian:</span> Choosing <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1</annotation></semantics></math> with full covariance <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> allows modeling correlations between components of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since a full parametrization is infeasible for high-dimensional tasks, we consider two approximations: <em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cholesky-based</em> and <em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">low-rank plus diagonal</em>, which are described in detail in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Approximations of the covariance matrix ‣ Appendix A Approximations of the noise distribution ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">All three model variants are compatible with the scoring rule framework, where we focus on <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.2 Theoretical justification ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), corresponding to a strictly proper scoring rule. For each case, we obtain closed-form expressions for all models in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Closed-form expressions for scoring rules ‣ Appendix A Approximations of the noise distribution ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>. Moreover, our parametrizations admit a closed-form backward distribution, which enables straightforward sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> be given as in Equation (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.3 Parametrization of 𝑝_𝜃^ϵ(⋅∣𝒙_𝑡) ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
Then the reverse distribution in Equation (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.2 Theoretical justification ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) admits the closed form</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{x}_{t-1};\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}+\lambda_{t}\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\gamma_{t}^{2}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{x}_{t-1};\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}+\lambda_{t}\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\gamma_{t}^{2}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}=\frac{\bm{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛍</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}=\frac{\bm{x}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}\coloneq\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}\coloneq\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}\coloneq\lambda_{t}-\frac{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}\coloneq\lambda_{t}-\frac{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The proof can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Approximations of the noise distribution ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this theorem to our univariate Gaussian instantiation and setting <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=\tilde{\beta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=\tilde{\beta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a DDPM model that learns the variance for each output dimension, similar to what was proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nichol and Dhariwal (2021)</a></cite>.
Remarkably, in both formulations, the variance is greater than <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\beta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\beta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notice, however, that our approach was derived from a principled way of modeling the diffusion noise as a normal distribution and can use any scoring rule as a loss function.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Numerical experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now evaluate the proposed methodology across several regression tasks where diffusion models have previously demonstrated competitive or even state-of-the-art performance. Specifically, we consider (i) UCI regression benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, (ii) autoregressive prediction tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasul et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohl et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Price et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and (iii) depth estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each task, we use our previously proposed instantiations: <em id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Univariate Gaussian</em> <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">univariate Gaussian mixture</em> <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mix}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>mix</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mix}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <em id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multivariate Gaussian</em> (<math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>mv</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mv}}</annotation></semantics></math>) with <em id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">low-rank plus diagonal</em> due to its superior performance, see Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Hyperparameters ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>. As a baseline, we use established diffusion models <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{\delta}_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{\delta}_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> from the literature.
In addition, we compare against the sample-based approach (<math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{t}^{\mathrm{ES}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ES</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{t}^{\mathrm{ES}}</annotation></semantics></math>) of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Where possible, we apply minor hyperparameter tuning (Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Hyperparameters ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>). Model performance is assessed using a variety of metrics, with particular emphasis on probabilistic calibration and distributional accuracy. Further experimental details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experiment details ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, and additional results and visualizations in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Additional results ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The code for the experiments in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 UCI regression ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Autoregressive prediction tasks ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> can be found in <a href="https://github.com/Philipp238/diffusionUQ" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/Philipp238/diffusionUQ</a> and for Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Monocular depth estimation ‣ 4 Numerical experiments ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> in <a href="https://github.com/Philipp238/Marigold" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/Philipp238/Marigold</a>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>UCI regression</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first evaluate our approach on the UCI regression benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dua and Graff, 2017</a>)</cite>, comparing it against CARD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which serves as the baseline <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additional details on CARD are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Details on CARD ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, and full experimental settings are given in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 UCI regression ‣ Appendix C Experiment details ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>RMSE (<math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) and CRPS (<math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) on the UCI datasets of the different methods.
For better readability, the results for Naval and Kin8nm have been scaled by a factor of 10000 and 100, respectively.</figcaption>
