<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Improving marginal calibration</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our method generally outperforms the standard diffusion model, especially on PDEs, it tends to be over-conservative, leading to overly high coverage and thus suboptimal marginal calibration. A simple remedy is to rescale the covariance matrix of <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}|\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}|\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the noise is always at least <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, adding a learned covariance can produce an overly dispersed distribution. Multiplying the covariance by a scalar reduces this spread and improves calibration. While this deviates from the DDIM framework and warrants further theoretical analysis, we show empirically that it can enhance both calibration and predictive performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.3 Parametrization of 𝑝_𝜃^ϵ(⋅∣𝒙_𝑡) ‣ 3 Our methodology ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have</p>
<table id="A5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}|\bm{x}_{t})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{x}_{t-1};\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\gamma_{t}^{2}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}|\bm{x}_{t})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta,k}\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{x}_{t-1};\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}}\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t-1}-\sigma_{t}^{2}}\bm{\mu}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\gamma_{t}^{2}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define the rescaled covariance as</p>
<table id="A5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tilde{}}{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}\coloneq\tau\left(\gamma_{t}^{2}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right),\quad\tau\in(0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mphantom></mphantom><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tilde{}}{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}\coloneq\tau\left(\gamma_{t}^{2}\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta,k}^{\epsilon}(\bm{x}_{t},t)+\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}\right),\quad\tau\in(0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the original covariance, while smaller <math id="A5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> reduces marginal variances. This adjustment can be applied to all diffusion methods, though for <math id="A5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{t}^{\mathrm{ES}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ES</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{t}^{\mathrm{ES}}</annotation></semantics></math> only the diagonal term <math id="A5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is rescaled. Figure <a href="#A5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ Appendix E Improving marginal calibration ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> illustrates the effect of <math id="A5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> on different metrics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F14" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A5.F14.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Burgers’</figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04583v1/Burgers_noise_tuning.svg" id="A5.F14.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/210;" width="476" height="210"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A5.F14.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Kuramoto–Sivashinsky</figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04583v1/KS_noise_tuning.svg" id="A5.F14.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/210;" width="476" height="210"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>Performance of the different diffusion models in dependence on the scaling parameter <math id="A5.F14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Results show that RMSE is typically minimized at <math id="A5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>, but for our proposed models (<math id="A5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mix}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>mix</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mix}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>mv</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{mv}}</annotation></semantics></math>), smaller values <math id="A5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\approx 0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\approx 0.05</annotation></semantics></math> improve coverage (close to <math id="A5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math>) and yield substantial gains in CRPS and energy score—up to a factor of two. For <math id="A5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> performance is best at <math id="A5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="A5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{t}^{\mathrm{ES}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ES</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{t}^{\mathrm{ES}}</annotation></semantics></math>, only CRPS and energy score show slight benefits for smaller <math id="A5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, rescaling the covariance offers a simple way to improve marginal calibration and predictive performance, though not uniformly across metrics. Choosing <math id="A5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> optimally remains an open problem, and introducing this parameter moves the model away from the DDIM framework. A theoretical study of the rescaled diffusion process offers a promising direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Capturing epistemic uncertainty</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When considering predictive uncertainty, one typically considers two sources of uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hüllermeier and Waegeman, 2021</a>]</cite>: <em id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">epistemic uncertainty</em> (EU) and <em id="A6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">aleatoric uncertainty</em> (AU). While aleatoric uncertainty describes the inherent randomness in the data-generating process, for example, due to measurement errors and is often referred to as <em id="A6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">irreducible</em> uncertainty, epistemic uncertainty arises from a lack of knowledge or information about the data-generating process and is also referred to as <em id="A6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reducible</em> uncertainty. While aleatoric uncertainty is naturally represented by a probability distribution, epistemic uncertainty usually requires a higher-order representation, such as a second-order distribution (distribution of a distribution). Although some works propose specialized diffusion architectures for estimation of EU<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berry et al., 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shu and Farimani, 2024</a>]</cite>, standard diffusion models generally lack such access.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our method addresses this by introducing an additional distribution <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> over the noise variable <math id="A6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which serves as a second-order distribution and enables direct estimation of epistemic uncertainty. Recall that for DDPMs, we have</p>
<table id="A6.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t-1};\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t-1}\mid\bm{x}_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{x}_{t-1};\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t),\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{x}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{x}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed diagonal <math id="A6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="A6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a deterministic denoiser. Iterative sampling then leads to the (approximate) predictive conditional distribution <math id="A6.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{Y}}(\cdot\mid\bm{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, from which aleatoric uncertainty can be assessed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shu and Farimani, 2024</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, by modeling <math id="A6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}^{\epsilon}(\epsilon_{t}\mid\bm{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, our approach induces a distribution over <math id="A6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> and hence a second-order distribution over the Gaussian mean in the transition kernel in (<a href="#A6.E56" title="In Appendix F Capturing epistemic uncertainty ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">56</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As an example, consider the diagonal covariance approximation, <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, EU can be estimated using the variance of the predictive mean:</p>
<table id="A6.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\mathbb{V}[\mathbb{E}[X]]}_{\mathrm{EU}}=\mathbb{V}[\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)]\propto\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>EU</mi></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\mathbb{V}[\mathbb{E}[X]]}_{\mathrm{EU}}=\mathbb{V}[\bm{\mu}_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{t},t)]\propto\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{\theta}^{2}(\bm{x}_{t},t)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, we focus on marginal epistemic uncertainty, i.e., one estimate per dimension <math id="A6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i=1,\ldots,D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i=1,\ldots,D)</annotation></semantics></math>, and average across timesteps <math id="A6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a single value for EU. With this construction, both AU and EU are expressed in the same space as the prediction target, provided that the diffusion process and input/output domain coincide.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F15" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A6.F15.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Kuramoto–Sivashinsky</figcaption><img src="2510.04583v1/KS_eu.png" id="A6.F15.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/121;" width="476" height="121" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A6.F15.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Burgers’</figcaption><img src="2510.04583v1/Burgers_eu.png" id="A6.F15.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/120;" width="476" height="120" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A6.F15.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>T2M</figcaption><img src="2510.04583v1/T2M_eu.png" id="A6.F15.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/228;" width="476" height="228" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span>Estimates of aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty for the <math id="A6.F15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}</annotation></semantics></math> model for the three different autoregressive prediction tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A6.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ Appendix F Capturing epistemic uncertainty ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> shows selected sample predictions and their corresponding uncertainty estimates for the three autoregressive regression tasks. For the Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equation, AU grows over time—consistent with its chaotic dynamics—while EU correlates with different solution branches, especially at early timesteps.
For the Burgers’ equation, both uncertainties remain small but vary with spatial location.
Finally, for surface temperature prediction, AU is elevated in high-altitude regions due to fine-scale variability, while EU highlights these regions even more strongly, with large values over the Alps where predicted temperatures are low. This suggests the model is less certain in these regimes, which could be mitigated by using additional training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, our model yields sensible EU estimates, but further theoretical and empirical study is needed—for example, exploring timestep aggregation strategies or alternative EU measures. These remain interesting directions for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Hyperparameters</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the high computational cost of the models, we are not able to provide an extensive statistical evaluation of all hyperparameters. However, since the proposed methods mainly affect the diffusion process, we can use the same model backbone for all methods and therefore still make them comparable. For some of the proposed methods, though, we introduce additional hyperparameters that need to be tuned. Therefore, we provide some minor hyperparameter evaluation for the prediction task of the Burgers’ and the Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equations. Table <a href="#A7.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix G Hyperparameters ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows the performance of the mixed normal model in dependence on the number of components <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, Table <a href="#A7.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix G Hyperparameters ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows the performance of the sample-based model in dependence on the number of samples <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and Table <a href="#A7.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix G Hyperparameters ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> shows the performance of the multivariate normal model in dependence on the covariance approximation and the kernel bandwidth <math id="A7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Each table shows the different metrics, as well as the average training time of one epoch. The training process is similar to the main experiment, but with more strict early stopping, to remain computationally feasible. All metrics are evaluated on an (unseen) validation set, and as a selection criterion, we choose the energy score.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>Effect of the number of components in the (univariate) mixture normal model on the performance on the validation set. For readability, the metrics RMSE, ES, and CRPS are scaled by the factor 100 (10) for the Burgers’ (KS) data.</figcaption>
<table id="A7.T11.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A7.T11.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A7.T11.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.75pt;padding-bottom:-0.75pt;">Experiment</td>
<td id="A7.T11.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.75pt;padding-bottom:-0.75pt;"><math id="A7.T11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A7.T11.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.75pt;padding-bottom:-0.75pt;"><math id="A7.T11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{epoch}[s]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{epoch}[s]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A7.T11.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.75pt;padding-bottom:-0.75pt;">RMSE<math id="A7.T11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A7.T11.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:-0.75pt;padding-bottom:-0.75pt;">ES<math id="A7.T11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
