<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>Comparison of scoring rules</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In principle, our framework allows for arbitrary scoring rule loss functions for training. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli et al. [2025]</a></cite> show that certain choices of <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the kernel score lead to loss functions that can recover the original diffusion loss in the limit, they also show empirical findings that scoring rules without this property can lead to similar results. To further motivate our use of the energy (and kernel) score, we show a brief comparison against a very common scoring rule, the log-score (or negative log-likelihood), which is defined as</p>
<table id="A7.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{log}}(p,\bm{y})\coloneq-\log p(\bm{y})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>log</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{log}}(p,\bm{y})\coloneq-\log p(\bm{y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for a probability density <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. The log-score is commonly used to train neural networks for parametric distributions such as predictive Gaussians <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nix and Weigend, 1994</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lakshminarayanan et al., 2017</a>]</cite>. We compare <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{log}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>log</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{log}}</annotation></semantics></math> against the energy score <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{ES}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ES</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{ES}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the two PDE prediction tasks and the <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\theta}^{\mathrm{diag}}</annotation></semantics></math> model, for which closed-form expressions are available for both loss functions. Table <a href="#A7.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ G.1 Comparison of scoring rules ‣ Appendix G Hyperparameters ‣ Improved probabilistic regression usingdiffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> shows that the energy score leads to significantly better results across all metrics for both PDEs. The performance of the model trained with <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{log}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>log</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{log}}</annotation></semantics></math> is up to a factor of 20 times worse, highlighting that training using the energy score can be advantageous, which goes along recent literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasp and Lerch, 2018</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and Meinshausen, 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alet et al., 2025</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T15" class="ltx_table">
